{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-adventure/1565064-one-off-adventure-setting-books","id":1565064,"title":"One off Adventure! Setting Books.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Adventure!"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-10-31T03:56:13-07:00","id":1565064,"title":"One off Adventure! Setting Books.","content":"Basically, these would be books were the <b>Adventure!</b> rules are used for a setting either tangential to the main <b>Adventure! </b>setting, or seperate from it. Tangential settings might involve distant planets, lost worlds, or areas of the main <b>Adventure! </b>setting that are functionally worlds of their own. A Tibet book for <b>Adventure! </b>would be describing a society so seperate from the rest of 1920s and 1930s Earth that it might as well be on another planet.<br /> <br /> An alternate history or a future (from the point of view of the 1919-1939 period) would be examples of seperate settings as would be settings completely distinct from the main book setting.<br /> <br /> Such books might require new rules. Example: A Sword and Sorcery setting (Connan is a Daredevil) would require new rules to allow more flexible knacks for <b>Mesmerists </b>and <b>Stalwarts</b>. This would allow them to be Sorcerers. A Space Opera setting would require Telepathy rules for all character types.<br /> <br /> Note: I realize that new books are unlikely. But speculation is fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-10-31T14:09:20-07:00","id":1565069,"title":"","content":"One idea that jumps to mind in the way of an alternate future for the <b>Adventure! </b>setting would be a post-apocalyptic setting. Two possibilities spring to mind first a physical cataclysm setting after an asteroid strike or a major Earth event. Both cometary collisions and massive seismic events were common apocalyptic events in pulp sci fi. The second option would be a plague which was also a commonplace in period fiction. <br /> <br /> I'd opt for the plague (some mad genius playing around with Spanish Flu and discovering that hubris is a real issue). This would allow the post-apocalyptic setting to be in the 1960s just when Pulp Fiction was getting rediscovered and reprinted. <br /> <br /> If you assume that there was a 90% die off (with Inspired Characters being immune). The population would just be beginning to become stable again. PCs from the 1930s campaigns could be active in the post-apocalyptic setting. You could even do a &quot;<b><i>set right what once went wrong&quot;</i></b> campaign. <br /> <br /> With decades of Inspired genius inventors the technology of the setting would be wild.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-10-31T14:15:18-07:00","id":1565070,"title":"","content":"Certain areas culturally seperate from the main game setting in the USA, Canada, Australia and Western Europe, could get useful books.<br /> <br /> China, Tibet, the British Raj, the Japanese Empire, the various sections of Africa and Latin America, Russia, Central Europe and Southeast and Maritime Asia could all justify books of their own.<br /> <br /> For that matter books on the USA, Australia, New Zealand, Canada, and Western Europe would be profoundly useful to those who don't know the period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-11-02T18:07:40-08:00","id":1565092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-adventure/1565064-one-off-adventure-setting-books?p=1565069#post1565069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd opt for the plague (some mad genius playing around with Spanish Flu and discovering that hubris is a real issue). This would allow the post-apocalyptic setting to be in the 1960s just when Pulp Fiction was getting rediscovered and reprinted.</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC, in one of his old Suppressed Transmissions columns, Ken Hite presented a potential pulp era horror setting in which the Spanish Influenza pandemic turned into a zombie apocalypse, and eventually Fu Manchu (or his non-name brand stand in) trying to invade North America with an army of jiangshi, and the US Army putting Herbert West in charge of coming up with countermeasures. <br /> <br /> Bruce Baugh (the original Adventure co-developer) suggested the idea of a pulp-hero American Revolution (and probably Napoleonic Wars) that led into a Steam-Tech/Steampunk American Civil War and eventually a space opera/sword &amp; planets Great War. <br /> <br /> I've also off and on toyed with the idea of moving the Aberrant era to WW2 and Aeon's set up to the 1960s. <br /> <br /> But I think the most useful sourcebook for any pulp game would probably be a Gazetteer/world tour book that looked at what was going on around the globe, including what sort of pulp or other odd fiction was being published in places like Japan, Germany, the Soviet Union, India, etc., as well as the various trope places (hidden cities in Africa, the Himalayas, and South America; the Hollow Earth, the lost Atlantis, Mu, Thule, etc.; alien ruins in Antarctica; the Moon, Mars, and Venus; and so on).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-11-06T17:12:24-08:00","id":1565160,"title":"","content":"Another one off setting (but expandable if it sells well) would be a Space Opera. Clark Ashton Smith had an intergalactic explorer character, Captain Volmar, in the 1920s. <br /> <br /> There would be two ways to do Space Opera in Adventure!. First, simply make Outer Space a separate area that the PCs can go to. Going above Earth's atmosphere would be like visiting a lost world. You could do a contemporary gazateer of the Solar System in the Adventure! Reality. <br /> <br /> Second, simply assume that the conditions that created the Adventure! Setting doesn't get stopped or unwound. WWII has a Space Race in it (steal from <b>Hard Vacuum </b>it isn't an RPG but it has WWII Space battles). The 1950s sees the exploration and settlement of the Moon. The 1960s and 70s see the exploration and settlement of the Solar System. The 1990s see interstellar travel open up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 11-06-2026, 08:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-11-07T13:42:24-08:00","id":1565171,"title":"","content":"Just as a side note. The kind of Time Travel adventures that the First and Second Doctors and their companions, certain runs of Tommy Tomorrow, H.G. Wells nameless time traveler, and those two lost guys in the <b>Time Tunnel </b>TV show had could and work with the <b>Adventure! </b>rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}